  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe core issue is the current algorithm's heavy reliance on local search techniques (simulated annealing and hill climbing) which frequently get stuck in local optima. The approach involves exploring truly novel function representations that exploit the problem's mathematical structure and introducing multi-scale optimization. We will:\n- Use Fourier-based expansions as a structured foundation with sparse coefficients\n- Implement hierarchical optimization across multiple resolutions (360, 720, 1440 points)\n- Employ multi-start strategies with basis projection operators\n- Use randomized basis pursuit for global exploration\n- Maintain a parent solution that can be up-sampled for coarse resolution starts\n\nThe rationale is that most existing methods rely on random search or simple heuristics, while the Erd\u0151s problem might be better addressed by functions with structured component interactions. Fourier representations are particularly suitable since the overlap involves correlation operations.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe parameterize the step function h(x) using a Fourier-Gaussian sparse representation:\n\n    h(x) = \u03c3\u2080(x) + \u03c3\u2081(x)cos(\u03c9\u2081x) + \u03c3\u2082(x)sin(\u03c9\u2082x)\n\nwhere \u03c3_i(x) are Gaussian windows:\n\n    \u03c3_i(x) = A_i * exp(-(x - \u03bc_i)\u00b2/\u03c3_i\u00b2)\n\nThe coefficients are optimized subject to:\n1. Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx=1\n2. Non-negativity: h(x)\u22650\n3. Bounding: h(x) \u22641\n\nThe objective is:\n   C5(\u03c9) = (2/n) * max_{shift=[-ceil(n/2), ceil(n/2)]} |\u222bh(x)h(x+shift)dx|\n\nUnlike the given method, we evaluate the continuous function with exact quadrature over 1002 points per resolution.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   We'll implement:\n   - A hierarchical resolution manager\n   - Fourier-series-based candidate generator\n   - Basis projection service for weights\n   - Coifman multi-scale optimization\n   \n   Pseudocode for run function:\n\n   ```python\n   def run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n      timekeeper = time.time\n      remaining = budget_s\n      n_parent = 720\n      resolutions = [1440, 720, 360]\n      best_c5 = float('inf')\n      best_h = None\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n\n      # Hierarchical resolution optimization\n      for res in resolutions:\n         points = res\n         local_budget = remaining * 0.4  # Cooldown budget\n         remaining -= local_budget\n        \n         # Build multiband candidate set\n         candidates = []\n         for _ in range(5):\n             # Randomized FourierSparse\n             N = 3  # Sparse coefficients\n             A = rng.normal(0.1, 0.5, 3*N+1)\n             centers = rng.uniform(0, 2, 3*N+1)\n             widths = np.abs(rng.normal(0.1, 0.2, 3*N+1))\n             phases = rng.uniform(0, \u03c0, 3*N+1)\n             for _ in range(20):\n                 A = np.clip(A + 0.1, 0.1, 2.0)  # Stabilize\n                 # ... construct candidate using sparseBasis\n                 h_cand = np.zeros(points)\n                 # Include parent-clone for each resolution\n                 if 'initial_h_values' in globals() and points*2 == n_parent and points in [360,720]:\n                     # Phase-adapted interp of parent at scale local to resolution\n                     # ...\n                     pass\n                 # Enforce integral and box constraints\n                 # Add to candidates\n                 candidates.append(h_cand)\n\n         # Multi-start optimization with phased inversions\n\n      # Basis expansion developement\n      class MultiScaleOptimizer:\n          def __init__(self):\n              self.operator = SparseFourierGrow()\n              self.scale_params = {\n                  '360': {'coeff_count': 5, 'freq_range': [1,3]},\n                  '720': {'coeff_count': 7, 'freq_range': [3,7]},\n                  '1440': {'coeff_count': 9, 'freq_range': [7,11]}\n          }\n          def optimize_for_scale(self, scale):\n              # Using conjugate gradient with probabilistic restarts\n              best_candidate = self.parent_solution # carryover from parent\n              # Attempt matrix-free optimization\n              return best_candidate\n\n      # Final intensive polishing at full 1440 resolution\n      # Downsample best result to 720 and return\n   ```\n\n   4. Search Choices and Variations\n      Core variations include:\n        - fft-sparse initialization\n        - Local descent (gradient-based)\n        - Gene",
